    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Antony and Cleopatra</span> is a tragedy by William Shakespeare, based on Plutarch's account of the love affair between the Roman general Mark Antony and Cleopatra VII, Queen of Egypt. Before studying the plot, a student must know the dramatis personae — the characters — and what each represents in the conflict between Rome (duty, empire, reason) and Egypt (passion, pleasure, excess).\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Triumvirs of Rome</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Mark Antony</td><td>One of the three rulers (triumvirs) of Rome; torn between duty to Rome and love for Cleopatra.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Octavius Caesar</td><td>Another triumvir; cold, calculating, ambitious; Antony's rival who becomes sole ruler of Rome.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Lepidus</td><td>The weakest of the three triumvirs; later removed from power by Caesar.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The Egyptian Court</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Cleopatra</td><td>Queen of Egypt; Antony's lover; passionate, witty, manipulative, and ultimately tragic in her suicide.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Charmian</td><td>Cleopatra's loyal attendant; dies beside her queen at the end of the play.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Iras</td><td>Another of Cleopatra's attendants; also dies with her queen.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mardian</td><td>A eunuch in Cleopatra's court.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Alexas</td><td>Cleopatra's attendant and messenger.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Key Supporting Characters</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Enobarbus</td><td>Antony's loyal friend and plain-speaking soldier; deserts Antony for Caesar but dies of grief and guilt.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Octavia</td><td>Caesar's sister; married to Antony as a political alliance; represents Roman virtue and is ultimately abandoned.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pompey (Sextus Pompeius)</td><td>Son of the great Pompey; challenges the triumvirs for power before making peace with them.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ventidius</td><td>A Roman general under Antony who defeats the Parthians.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Agrippa and Maecenas</td><td>Friends and supporters of Octavius Caesar.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The Soothsayer</td><td>An Egyptian fortune-teller who warns Antony that Caesar's fortune will always overshadow his.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Symbolic Function of the Characters</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Shakespeare uses the characters to embody the play's central conflict: <strong>Rome versus Egypt</strong>. Caesar, Octavia, and the Roman soldiers represent duty, discipline, and reason. Cleopatra, her court, and the pleasures of Egypt represent passion, sensuality, and excess. Antony is caught between the two worlds, and his tragedy lies in his inability to reconcile them.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> WAEC frequently asks candidates to describe a character's role or to compare two characters (e.g., Cleopatra and Octavia, or Enobarbus and Antony). Know each character's loyalty, and what they represent in the Rome-versus-Egypt contrast.\n</div>\n",
