    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Act I introduces the central conflict of the play: Antony's divided loyalty between the duties of Rome and his passion for Cleopatra in Egypt.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Opening Scene — Philo's Complaint</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The play opens with the Roman soldier <strong>Philo</strong> complaining to Demetrius that Antony's love for Cleopatra has made him neglect his duties as a soldier and triumvir. Philo describes Antony's eyes, which once looked like Mars surveying war, as now doting on a \"tawny front\" (Cleopatra). This sets the Rome-versus-Egypt theme from the very first lines.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Antony and Cleopatra's Love Display</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Antony declares that his love for Cleopatra cannot be measured — <strong>\"there's beggary in the love that can be reckoned.\"</strong> Cleopatra teases and tests him by asking how much he loves her, and mocks the arrival of messengers from Rome, urging Antony to hear the news. Antony dismisses Rome, declaring \"Let Rome in Tiber melt,\" showing he is (at this point) choosing Egypt over duty.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. News from Rome</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>News</th><th>Significance</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Fulvia (Antony's wife) has died</td><td>Removes one obstacle to Antony's freedom, but also a reminder of his neglected Roman responsibilities.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sextus Pompeius is gathering forces at sea</td><td>A military threat to the triumvirate that demands Antony's attention.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fulvia and Antony's brother had waged war against Caesar</td><td>Shows the political instability Antony has ignored while in Egypt.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Antony's Decision to Return to Rome</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">After hearing the news, Antony resolves to break free of Egypt's \"strong Egyptian fetters\" and return to Rome, admitting that he \"must from this enchanting queen break off.\" This shows the beginning of his internal struggle — duty calling him back even as his heart remains with Cleopatra.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Cleopatra's Reaction and Enobarbus's Commentary</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Cleopatra is furious and heartbroken when Antony announces his departure, accusing him of never truly loving her and mocking his excuses. <strong>Enobarbus</strong>, Antony's blunt-speaking friend, offers a cynical, comic commentary throughout, observing that Cleopatra's moods and \"infinite variety\" make her impossible to predict or resist.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Key Theme in Act I:</strong> The conflict between <em>reason and passion</em>, and between <em>public duty and private love</em>, is established immediately. Antony is torn from the play's opening moments.\n</div>\n",
