    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Acts IV and V bring the tragedy to its resolution: Enobarbus's death from guilt, Antony's final battles and suicide, and Cleopatra's own dignified death.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Enobarbus's Desertion and Death</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Having lost faith in Antony's judgement, <strong>Enobarbus deserts</strong> to Caesar's camp. Antony, learning of this, generously and without anger sends Enobarbus's belongings and treasure after him. Overwhelmed with guilt and shame at his own disloyalty in the face of such generosity, Enobarbus dies of a broken heart, calling out Antony's name — a symbol of the destructive cost of the Rome/Egypt conflict even on loyal men.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Antony's Temporary Land Victory</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">On land, Antony achieves a brief victory against Caesar's forces and returns to Cleopatra triumphant and hopeful, praising his soldiers. This gives a short-lived rise in tension before the final catastrophe.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Final Defeat at Sea and Antony's Accusation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">In the final naval battle, Antony's Egyptian fleet surrenders to Caesar without a fight. Believing Cleopatra has betrayed him and sold him out to Caesar, Antony furiously denounces her as a \"triple-turned whore\" and vows revenge.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Cleopatra's False Death Message</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Terrified of Antony's rage, Cleopatra locks herself in her monument and sends word through her attendant <strong>Mardian</strong> that she has killed herself, her last word supposedly being \"Antony.\" She intended this to soften Antony's anger, but it produces a far more tragic result.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Antony's Suicide</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Devastated by the false news of Cleopatra's death, Antony asks his loyal servant <strong>Eros</strong> to kill him, but Eros kills himself instead rather than harm his master. Antony then falls on his own sword but fails to die instantly. He learns Cleopatra is alive and is carried, dying, to her monument, where he dies in her arms after urging her to seek safety with Caesar only on her own honourable terms.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Cleopatra's Meeting with Caesar</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Caesar treats Cleopatra with outward courtesy but secretly plans to parade her as a captive in his triumph in Rome — a humiliation Cleopatra is determined to avoid at all costs. Cleopatra tests Caesar's intentions and confirms, through his steward Seleucus's forced testimony about her hidden treasure, that Caesar cannot be trusted.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">7. Cleopatra's Suicide</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Choosing death over dishonour, Cleopatra dresses in her royal robes and crown and applies poisonous asps (snakes) smuggled in a basket of figs to her arm and breast. Her attendants <strong>Charmian</strong> and <strong>Iras</strong> die alongside her. Charmian's final act is to straighten Cleopatra's crown before she too dies, declaring the death \"well done.\"</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Key Themes:</strong> Loyalty and betrayal (Enobarbus), the destructive power of jealousy (Antony's suicide), and dignity in death (Cleopatra choosing suicide over Roman humiliation) all converge in the tragic resolution.\n</div>\n",
