    "module": "Literary Appreciation and Exam Skills",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  In the WASSCE Literature-in-English paper, candidates are given a short passage of prose they have never seen before (\"unseen prose\") and asked questions testing comprehension, analysis, and appreciation — without relying on memorised knowledge of a set text.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. What Unseen Prose Tests</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>What It Involves</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Comprehension</td><td>Understanding what literally happens in the passage.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Plot analysis</td><td>Identifying the sequence and significance of events.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Character analysis</td><td>Inferring a character's traits from their words, actions, and thoughts.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Setting</td><td>Identifying where and when the extract takes place, and its effect.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tone and mood</td><td>The writer's attitude and the atmosphere created for the reader.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Style and language</td><td>Word choice, sentence structure, and figurative language used.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Strategy for Approaching Unseen Prose</h3>\n<ol style=\"margin-left:20px;\">\n  <li>Read the passage through once fully, without stopping, to grasp the general sense.</li>\n  <li>Read the questions carefully before re-reading the passage a second time.</li>\n  <li>Underline or note key words, phrases, and details relevant to the questions.</li>\n  <li>Answer using evidence from the passage — avoid inventing details not supported by the text.</li>\n  <li>Support points with brief, relevant references to the passage rather than long quotations.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Common Question Types</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  Typical unseen prose questions ask candidates to: summarise a section in their own words; explain the meaning of specific words/phrases as used in context; describe a character's feelings or motivation; identify and explain a literary device (e.g., simile, irony, flashback); or comment on the effectiveness of the writer's technique.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Avoiding Common Mistakes</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Candidates often lose marks by: answering from memory of other stories instead of the given passage; copying long, unexplained quotations instead of analysing them; misreading the question's specific instruction (e.g., \"in your own words\"); and giving an answer that is too general to earn marks tied to the passage's specific content.</p>\n",
