    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Alongside unseen prose, candidates are also given a short poem they have not studied before, and are tested on their ability to interpret meaning, structure, and poetic devices independently.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Features to Examine in an Unseen Poem</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What to Look For</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Subject matter</td><td>What the poem is literally about, on the surface.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Theme</td><td>The deeper message or idea the poet is exploring.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Structure</td><td>Stanza pattern, line length, rhyme scheme, and how form relates to meaning.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Imagery</td><td>Descriptive, sensory language that creates pictures in the reader's mind.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mood/tone</td><td>The emotional atmosphere and the speaker's attitude.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sound devices</td><td>Rhyme, rhythm, alliteration, and assonance, and their effect.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Common Literary Devices in Unseen Poems</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Metaphor</td><td>A direct comparison between two unlike things without using \"like\" or \"as\".</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Simile</td><td>A comparison using \"like\" or \"as\".</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Personification</td><td>Giving human qualities to non-human things or ideas.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Irony</td><td>A contrast between expectation and reality, or between what is said and what is meant.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Alliteration</td><td>Repetition of the same initial consonant sound in nearby words.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Paradox</td><td>A statement that seems self-contradictory but reveals a deeper truth.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Symbolism</td><td>Using an object or image to represent a larger idea.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Strategy for Approaching an Unseen Poem</h3>\n<ol style=\"margin-left:20px;\">\n  <li>Read the poem slowly, at least twice — poems reward re-reading more than prose does.</li>\n  <li>Identify the speaker (persona) — who is \"speaking\" the poem, and to whom?</li>\n  <li>Note the title, since it often gives a clue to the theme.</li>\n  <li>Look for the \"turn\" — a shift in tone, focus, or argument, often near the end.</li>\n  <li>Always explain the effect of a device you identify, not just name it.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Never just say \"the poet uses a simile.\" Say what is being compared, and explain what effect or meaning that comparison creates — examiners award marks for interpretation, not just identification.\n</div>\n",
