    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  WASSCE Literature-in-English questions often use specific instructional verbs. Understanding exactly what each verb demands is essential to scoring well, especially in context questions drawn directly from set texts.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of a Context Question</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">context question</span> presents a short extract or passage taken directly from a set text (prose, drama, or poetry) and asks the candidate to answer questions based on the surrounding circumstances — who is speaking, to whom, where, and why — as well as the passage's meaning and significance.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Common Instructional (Exam) Verbs</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>What It Requires</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Comment on</td><td>Give a reasoned opinion or observation, supported with evidence.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Describe</td><td>Give a detailed, accurate account of something.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Contrast</td><td>Point out the differences between two things.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Compare</td><td>Point out similarities (and often differences) between two things.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Examine</td><td>Investigate a subject in detail, considering different aspects.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Discuss</td><td>Explore a topic from multiple angles, weighing points for and against.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Justify</td><td>Give reasons or evidence to support a claim or judgement.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Illustrate</td><td>Use examples to make a point clear.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Structure for a Context Question Answer</h3>\n<ol style=\"margin-left:20px;\">\n  <li><strong>Context:</strong> Identify the speaker, the listener, and the situation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Meaning:</strong> Paraphrase or explain what is being said in the extract.</li>\n  <li><strong>Significance:</strong> Explain the extract's importance to plot, character, or theme.</li>\n  <li><strong>Device/Style (if asked):</strong> Identify any notable literary device and its effect.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Developing Accurate, Concise Responses</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  Concise answers that directly address the exact verb used score higher than long, vague answers. If a question says \"contrast,\" simply describing both things without stating their differences will lose marks — always answer the verb, not just the topic.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Common Errors to Avoid</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Avoid: retelling the whole plot instead of answering the specific question asked; ignoring the instructional verb (e.g., writing a description when asked to \"justify\"); failing to use the extract itself as evidence; and writing answers with no clear structure or organisation.</p>\n",
