    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic consolidates the whole of <strong>Antony and Cleopatra</strong>, drawing together characterisation, plot development, major conflicts, and the moral lessons the play offers to a modern audience.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Characterisation Review</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Defining Trait</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Antony</td><td>A once-great soldier undone by passion; torn between duty and love; ultimately noble in his final loyalty to Cleopatra.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cleopatra</td><td>Complex, theatrical, and politically astute; capable of genuine love but also of manipulation; dies with dignity.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Octavius Caesar</td><td>Calculating, ambitious, and self-controlled; represents cold political reason over passion.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Enobarbus</td><td>Loyal but pragmatic; his desertion and death show the tragic cost of choosing self-preservation over loyalty.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Plot Development — The Arc of the Tragedy</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The play moves from Antony's initial neglect of Rome for Egypt (Act I), through his temporary reconciliation with Caesar via marriage to Octavia (Act II), to the catastrophic choice to follow Cleopatra at Actium (Act III), and finally to the deaths of Enobarbus, Antony, and Cleopatra (Acts IV–V). Each act deepens the central conflict between reason/duty (Rome) and passion/pleasure (Egypt).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Major Conflicts</h3>\n<ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\">\n  <li><strong>Internal conflict:</strong> Antony's struggle between his Roman identity and his love for Cleopatra.</li>\n  <li><strong>External conflict:</strong> The political and military struggle between Antony and Octavius Caesar for control of Rome.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rome versus Egypt:</strong> The symbolic conflict between discipline/empire and passion/pleasure that runs through the entire play.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Moral Lessons for Modern Society</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  The play warns against letting personal passion override duty and sound judgement, especially for those in positions of leadership. It also shows the destructive power of jealousy and hasty decisions (Antony's rage against Cleopatra), the cost of political ambition (Caesar's ruthless pursuit of sole power), and the human capacity for dignity even in defeat (Cleopatra's chosen death over humiliation).\n</div>\n",
