    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic covers the two prescribed African prose texts: Pede Hollist's <strong>So the Path Does Not Die</strong> and Elma Shaw's <strong>Redemption Road</strong>, both of which confront painful chapters of West African social history through the eyes of resilient women.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. So the Path Does Not Die — Pede Hollist</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Set largely in Sierra Leone and the United States, the novel follows <strong>Finaba (\"Fina\") Marah</strong>, a girl whose traditional female initiation (circumcision) ceremony is interrupted before it can be completed. Fina emigrates to America, where she builds a new, \"fashionable\" life in Washington, D.C., but is haunted by her broken initiation and her sense of not fully belonging in either world. When Sierra Leone's civil war breaks out, she is drawn back home to search for her grandmother; unable to find her, Fina instead stays to help traumatised women and girls who survived the war, symbolically completing — and redefining — the \"path\" that was interrupted in her own youth.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Protagonist</td><td>Finaba \"Fina\" Marah</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Setting</td><td>Rural Sierra Leone, Freetown, and Washington, D.C., USA</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key themes</td><td>Female circumcision/initiation, tradition vs. change, diaspora and \"otherness,\" war, healing and restoration</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Narrative structure</td><td>A bildungsroman (coming-of-age story) that begins and ends in Africa</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Redemption Road — Elma Shaw</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Set in Monrovia, Liberia, during and after Charles Taylor's rule, the novel follows <strong>Bendu Lewis</strong>, a young woman who counsels other survivors of Liberia's civil war while privately struggling with trauma from her own wartime experiences. When the warlord who once held her captive — known as <strong>Commander Cobra</strong> — resurfaces in her town, Bendu resolves to pursue justice for his past atrocities, a quest that forces her to finally confront and make peace with her own painful, long-hidden wartime secret.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Protagonist</td><td>Bendu Lewis</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Antagonist</td><td>Commander Cobra, a former warlord</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Setting</td><td>Post-war Monrovia, Liberia</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key themes</td><td>Trauma and healing, justice and accountability, atonement, the legacy of civil war, resilience of women</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Comparing the Two Novels</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  Both novels centre on strong West African women confronting deeply painful personal and cultural wounds — Fina's interrupted initiation and Bendu's wartime trauma — and both end with their protagonists choosing to help other women and girls, transforming personal pain into a source of healing for their communities.\n</div>\n",
