    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic covers Harper Lee's classic American novel <strong>To Kill a Mockingbird</strong> and Susanne Bellefeuille's biographical novel <strong>Path of Lucas: The Journey He Endured</strong>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. To Kill a Mockingbird — Harper Lee</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Set in the fictional town of <strong>Maycomb, Alabama</strong>, during the 1930s, the novel is narrated by <strong>Jean Louise \"Scout\" Finch</strong>, who recalls her childhood with her older brother Jem and their friend Dill. Their father, <strong>Atticus Finch</strong>, a principled lawyer, defends <strong>Tom Robinson</strong>, a Black man falsely accused of raping a white woman, Mayella Ewell. Despite clear evidence of Tom's innocence, an all-white jury convicts him, reflecting the deep racial injustice of the American South. A parallel plot follows the children's fascination with their reclusive neighbour, <strong>Boo Radley</strong>, who ultimately saves Jem and Scout from an attack by Mayella's vengeful father, Bob Ewell.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Narrator/Protagonist</td><td>Scout Finch</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key characters</td><td>Atticus Finch, Jem Finch, Tom Robinson, Boo Radley, Bob and Mayella Ewell</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Setting</td><td>Maycomb, Alabama, USA, during the Great Depression</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key themes</td><td>Racial injustice, loss of innocence, moral courage, empathy (\"walking in someone else's skin\"), prejudice</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Title symbolism</td><td>A \"mockingbird\" symbolises innocent beings who are harmed for doing no wrong — such as Tom Robinson and Boo Radley.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Path of Lucas: The Journey He Endured — Susanne Bellefeuille</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Based on true events from the author's own family, the novel follows <strong>Lucas Clarkson</strong>, a hardworking farmer's son from a small town in Eastern Ontario, Canada, who becomes a successful mechanic. Facing a family crisis after his daughter <strong>Lucy</strong> is left comatose by a car crash, Lucas recounts his life's story — including his courtship of his wife <strong>Isabelle</strong> — in a desperate attempt to reach and anchor his unconscious daughter back to the world of the living.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Protagonist</td><td>Lucas Clarkson</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key characters</td><td>Isabelle (Lucas's wife), Lucy (their comatose daughter)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Setting</td><td>A small farming town in Eastern Ontario, Canada</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key themes</td><td>Family devotion, perseverance through hardship, love and sacrifice, storytelling as a means of healing</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Basis</td><td>Based on true events in the author's own family; Lucas is modelled on her father and Isabelle on her mother</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Both novels use a personal, close narrative voice — Scout's child narrator in Mockingbird and Lucas's own retrospective storytelling — to explore how ordinary families endure extraordinary hardship.\n</div>\n",
