    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic covers Efua Sutherland's Ghanaian classic <strong>The Marriage of Anansewa</strong> and Bosede Ademilua-Afolayan's Nigerian play <strong>Once Upon an Elephant</strong>, both rooted in traditional African storytelling techniques.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The Marriage of Anansewa — Efua Sutherland</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Written in the tradition of Akan \"Anansesem\" (spider/Ananse storytelling), the play follows <strong>Ananse</strong>, a poor but cunning man, who tries to secure his fortune by promising his daughter <strong>Anansewa</strong> in marriage to four wealthy chiefs at once, hoping to collect dowry gifts from each without any of them discovering the deception. His scheme is complicated when all four suitors arrive at the same time, forcing Ananse into increasingly desperate lies — including faking Anansewa's death — to avoid exposure. The play uses a <strong>storyteller (the \"Player\")</strong> who directly narrates and comments on the action, breaking the fourth wall in the tradition of oral storytelling.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Protagonist</td><td>Ananse (Kwaku Ananse)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key character</td><td>Anansewa, his daughter</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Genre/style</td><td>A \"storytelling drama\" — combines Western dramatic form with Akan oral storytelling conventions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key themes</td><td>Greed and cunning, the commodification of marriage, deception, satire of materialism in modern society</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Once Upon an Elephant — Bosede Ademilua-Afolayan</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Using Yoruba oral traditions and folktale techniques, the play tells of <strong>Ajanaku (Olaniyonu)</strong>, who is not the rightful heir to the throne but ascends it with the help of the kingmaker and chief priest, <strong>Serubawon</strong>. Ajanaku's reign becomes tyrannical, marked by terror and abuse of power; his crimes culminate in the rape of the virgin <strong>Desola</strong> as part of a ritual he believes will grant him immortality. This atrocity sets in motion his eventual downfall, as his insatiable lust for power, wealth, and fame ultimately leads to his death.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Protagonist/Antagonist</td><td>Ajanaku (Olaniyonu), the tyrant king</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key characters</td><td>Serubawon (kingmaker/chief priest), Desola (his victim), Akinjobi (the previous king)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Style</td><td>Uses Yoruba folklore and oral storytelling conventions to critique dictatorship</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key themes</td><td>Tyranny and abuse of power, \"sit-tightism\" (leaders refusing to relinquish power), corruption, justice and downfall of dictators</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Comparing the Plays:</strong> Both playwrights draw on indigenous African oral storytelling forms — Sutherland uses Akan Anansesem and a narrating \"Player,\" while Ademilua-Afolayan uses Yoruba folktale conventions — to critique universal human failings: greed in Anansewa, and tyranny in Once Upon an Elephant.\n</div>\n",
