    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic covers J. B. Priestley's <strong>An Inspector Calls</strong> and Robert Bolt's <strong>A Man for All Seasons</strong>, two plays that explore themes of social responsibility, integrity, and conscience.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. An Inspector Calls — J. B. Priestley</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Set in 1912, the play centres on the wealthy <strong>Birling family</strong>, whose celebratory dinner is interrupted by the mysterious <strong>Inspector Goole</strong>, investigating the suicide of a young working-class woman, <strong>Eva Smith</strong> (also known as Daisy Renton). One by one, the Inspector reveals that every member of the family — Arthur Birling, Sybil Birling, their children Sheila and Eric, and Sheila's fiancé Gerald Croft — contributed, through selfishness or cruelty, to the chain of events that drove Eva to her death. The play ends ambiguously, questioning whether the Inspector was real, but reinforcing that the family's moral guilt remains regardless.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Key characters</td><td>Inspector Goole, Arthur & Sybil Birling, Sheila, Eric, Gerald Croft, Eva Smith/Daisy Renton</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Setting</td><td>The Birling family's dining room, 1912, England</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key themes</td><td>Social responsibility, class inequality, guilt and denial, generational divide (the young accept responsibility; the old do not)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Dramatic technique</td><td>A \"well-made play\" structure with a single set and a mysterious investigator device</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. A Man for All Seasons — Robert Bolt</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The play dramatises the true story of <strong>Sir Thomas More</strong>, Lord Chancellor of England under King Henry VIII, who refuses to publicly support the King's divorce from Catherine of Aragon and his establishment of the Church of England, because doing so would violate More's Catholic conscience. Despite immense pressure from the King, his family, and even friends like the ambitious <strong>Richard Rich</strong> (who eventually betrays him with false testimony), More refuses to compromise his principles, choosing silence rather than an outright lie. He is ultimately tried, convicted through perjured evidence, and executed for treason.</p>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Protagonist</td><td>Sir Thomas More</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key characters</td><td>King Henry VIII, Richard Rich, Thomas Cromwell, the Common Man (a narrator figure who plays multiple minor roles)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Setting</td><td>16th-century England, during the reign of Henry VIII</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Key themes</td><td>Integrity and conscience, the danger of political ambition, the individual versus the state, hypocrisy</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Comparing the Plays:</strong> Both plays explore what happens when personal integrity clashes with social or political pressure. The Birlings choose denial over responsibility, while Thomas More chooses death over compromising his conscience — offering a striking moral contrast.\n</div>\n",
