    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic reviews all twelve prescribed poems — six African and six non-African — focusing on their themes, tone, and poetic devices.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. African Poetry</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Poem &amp; Poet</th><th>Theme / Focus</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>\"Once Upon a Time\" — Gabriel Okara</td><td>A father laments the loss of sincerity and warmth in adult life, contrasting fake modern \"face\" culture with his son's innocent openness; he longs to relearn genuine laughter.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"New Tongue\" — Elizabeth L. A. Kamara</td><td>A new generation abandons traditional language, customs, and family ties in favour of a \"borrowed\" foreign culture, symbolising the erosion of heritage.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"Night\" — Wole Soyinka</td><td>A dense, sensuous meditation on night as both a lover and a threatening presence, blending imagery of the sea, shadows, and hidden fears.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"Not My Business\" — Niyi Osundare</td><td>A satirical warning against apathy toward injustice and political persecution — the speaker's indifference to others' suffering ends when he himself becomes a victim.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"Hearty Garlands\" — S.O.H. Afriyie-Vidza</td><td>A celebratory birthday tribute to an elderly, accomplished mentor, honouring a long and fulfilled life at eighty-five.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"The Breast of the Sea\" — Syl Cheney-Coker</td><td>Uses the image of the polluted, suffering sea to reflect on history's violence and Sierra Leone's own wounds, including war and exploitation.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Non-African Poetry</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Poem &amp; Poet</th><th>Theme / Focus</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>\"She Walks in Beauty\" — Lord Byron</td><td>A tribute to a woman's perfect physical and inner beauty, comparing her to a cloudless, starry night.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"The Nun's Priest's Tale\" (shortened) — Geoffrey Chaucer</td><td>A mock-heroic beast fable about a proud rooster, Chanticleer, and his favourite hen, Pertelote, satirising human vanity and pride through animal characters.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"Digging\" — Seamus Heaney</td><td>The poet compares his pen and writing to his father's and grandfather's manual work with a spade, honouring their labour while affirming his own different path.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"Still I Rise\" — Maya Angelou</td><td>A defiant, empowering declaration of resilience against oppression, racism, and attempts to diminish the speaker's dignity.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"The Telephone Call\" — Fleur Adcock</td><td>A satirical poem about a hoax lottery call, exposing how easily people are manipulated by the promise of sudden wealth.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>\"The Stone\" — Wilfrid Wilson Gibson</td><td>A grieving woman, turned numb with shock after her lover's death in a quarry accident, watches in silence as a stonecutter carves his name on a gravestone.</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Comparative Notes</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  Group poems by shared themes for revision: loss of tradition/identity (\"Once Upon a Time,\" \"New Tongue\"), social/political injustice (\"Not My Business,\" \"The Breast of the Sea,\" \"Still I Rise\"), grief and loss (\"The Stone\"), and celebration/tribute (\"Hearty Garlands,\" \"She Walks in Beauty,\" \"Digging\").\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Common Poetic Devices to Identify</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Look for: <strong>refrain</strong> (Osundare's repeated question after each stanza), <strong>extended metaphor</strong> (Heaney's pen-as-spade; Cheney-Coker's sea-as-body), <strong>satire and irony</strong> (Adcock's fake lottery call; Chaucer's mock-heroic tone), and <strong>anaphora/repetition</strong> for emphasis (Angelou's repeated declaration of rising).</p>\n",
